.he "permalloc (3)                8/11/26                permalloc (3)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
SUBROUTINE(S)
.in +5
.br
.bd
permalloc, permdup
- allocation that is never given back
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
char *permalloc(n)
.br
unsigned int n;
.sp
.bd 2
char *permdup(s)
.br
char *s;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Permalloc
returns
.bd
n
bytes, aligned for a pointer and set to zero.
.bd
Permdup
copies the string
.bd
s
into fresh storage, packed tight and not aligned, and returns it.

Neither can be freed.  That is the point of them: a symbol table, a
type node, an interned spelling - anything that lives until the
program exits - has no use for malloc's per-block header or its free
list, and on a machine with 64K those are worth more than the
generality.  Both carve their result out of a chunk taken from
.bd
malloc
(see section 3), and take another chunk when the current one runs
out, so the only waste is the few bytes left at the end of each.

The chunks are chained through their first word, so the arena stays
walkable.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
Running out of memory is fatal.  Both routines report
.bd
out of memory
on the standard error and exit; there is no failure return, because
there is nothing a caller on this machine could usefully do with
one.  A program that can genuinely carry on without the storage
should call
.bd
__malloc
(see
.bd
malloc,
section 3) and look at what it gets.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
For programs built for Micronix these live in
.bd
libc.a.
The host build of the compiler links them from
.bd
libccc.a
instead, from the same source, so that the passes have them whether
they are running on the machine or building for it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
